Serialize parser_ll_c behind parser_yy_c to avoid a parallel-build race.

CMake declares parser_yy.c / parser_ly.h as outputs of the parser_yy_c
target. The flex custom_command for parser_ll_c lists parser_ly.h in its
DEPENDS, which pulls the parser_yy.c rule into parser_ll_c.dir/build.make
as well as parser_yy_c.dir/build.make. Under `make -j`, both submakes
schedule the shared rule concurrently and race on the destination —
manifesting on bison-less hosts as a duplicated "Generating parser_yy.c,
parser_ly.h" line followed by:

    Error copying file ".../last_resort/parser_ly.h" to
      ".../build/src/libscotch/.": No such file or directory

Adding a target-level dependency forces make to finish parser_yy_c
(populating parser_yy.c / parser_ly.h in the binary dir) before
parser_ll_c starts. Once the files exist, the no-prereq copy rule in
parser_ll_c's submake is skipped, and the race disappears.

--- a/src/libscotch/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/libscotch/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -143,6 +143,7 @@ else()
     COMMAND ${CMAKE_COMMAND} -E copy "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/last_resort/parser_ll.c" "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/last_resort/parser_lh.h" "${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/.")
 endif(FLEX_FOUND)
 add_custom_target(parser_ll_c DEPENDS ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/parser_ll.c ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/parser_lh.h)
+add_dependencies(parser_ll_c parser_yy_c)

